# Decisions log

One dated line per decision that changes the design. Newest at the bottom.

- 2026-10-29: Keep the Next.js `app/` directory at the repo root (no `src/`). Other folders (`components/`, `features/`, `lib/`, `types/`) also live at the root; the `@/*` alias maps to `./*`.
- 2026-10-29: Next 16 replaces `middleware.ts` with `proxy.ts`. Session refresh (task 1.7) goes in a root-level `proxy.ts`.
- 2026-10-29: Removed `cacheComponents` and `partialPrefetching` from `next.config.ts`; neither was intentional and Cache Components changes how cookies and Supabase calls behave.
- 2026-10-29: Local development points at the local Supabase stack via `.env.local`; hosted project values live only in Vercel env vars.
- 2026-10-29: Bumped `@types/node` to `^22` (matches the Node 22 runtime; Vitest 5 requires `^22 || >=24`, and npm refuses to install with `^20`).
- 2026-10-31: Task 0.5 embedding decision: `gemini-embedding-2` at 768 dimensions (`vector(768)` stays), with no additional L2 normalization (768-dimension vectors measured unit length; Google documents automatic normalization for reduced dimensions). The adapter applies the document prefix `title: none | text: {text}` at ingest and the query prefix `task: search result | query: {text}` at search time (no `taskType`), and the exact model id is stored per chunk in `chunks.embedding_model`. Evidence: `docs/spikes/provider.md` section 5.6 (USER-verified, not independently file-inspected). Not decided here: quota accounting (and the quota behind the unpaced-run 429s) and real-PDF ingest time.
- 2026-10-31: Scope of provider use (Task 0.5): free-tier terms for Gemini and Groq were reviewed; free-tier use is acceptable for this personal portfolio project during development with synthetic or non-sensitive documents. If it becomes a real product, reassess paid-tier privacy and data-handling requirements. LLM roles remain provisional: Gemini primary, Groq fallback.
- 2026-10-31: Task 0.4 (CI) closed. GitHub Actions (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs install, lint, typecheck, unit tests and build on Node 22, with the version pinned via `actions/setup-node` in the workflow. Verified: a normal run passed all steps; a deliberately broken assertion in `tests/unit/spikes-provider.test.ts` failed the Unit tests step (then restored, local tests passing); the latest run on `test/ci-red-check` is green. Not tested here: pull-request-triggered runs, Playwright or the Supabase stack in CI.
- 2026-11-01: Task 0.6 (PDF extraction) closed. Library: `unpdf` 1.9.1 (bundles pdf.js 6.4.299), pinned below 2.x because 2.x declares Node 24 or newer while CI and local use Node 22. Verified on Vercel (function on Node v26.13.0, `iad1`) with 12 synthetic fixtures: correct per-page text on single-column, table, 30-page and owner-password-only PDFs; both scanned files flagged and no text file flagged with a threshold of 25 average non-whitespace characters per page (scans 0 and 5, sparsest text 59); user-password, corrupt and non-PDF inputs return controlled errors (`encrypted`, `corrupt`, `not_pdf`) and the instance kept serving. Measured: 30-page warm extraction 98.7 to 182.9 ms (3 runs); one cold sample 316.3 ms server-side on a 10-page file. Known limit: line-interleaved two-column text is not recoverable (0 of 14 fragments on those pages). Evidence: `docs/spikes/pdf.md`. Not decided: behaviour on real PDFs, the threshold on real scans, owner-password-only PDFs, a fix for interleaved columns, and Node 22 (CI) versus 26 (Vercel).
- 2026-11-01: Task 0.7 (citation highlighting) spike done. Direct highlighting is feasible. Claude's automated verification (headless Chromium 153, production build via `next build` then `next start`): single-column 5 of 5, table 5 of 5, two-column 3 of 5 (the 2 misses are line-interleaved pages, unrecoverable as in 0.6), with 0 wrong highlights; a separate edge fixture (ligatures, hyphens, curly quotes) 5 of 5 and not counted. Separately user-confirmed: the project owner tested the spike by hand in their own browser in development and production (`yarn build && yarn start`) and highlights appeared there too and everything else worked, including inline error handling (no scores recorded for that run). The 40% gate was not triggered, so build direct highlight first with page jump plus passage panel as the mandatory fallback whenever the matcher declines (unique match only, 12-character minimum). Only the page jump exists in the spike; the panel is Task 3.6. `react-pdf` 11.0.0 and `pdfjs-dist` 6.3.289 pinned exactly; `Document` and `Page` need `suspense={false}` (or an error boundary) or load errors become a full-page crash. Evidence: `docs/spikes/highlight.md`. Not decided or tested: real PDFs, cross-browser coverage (the owner's browser is not recorded), Vercel deployment, chunk text from `unpdf` (PDF.js 6.4.299) versus client PDF.js 6.3.289.
- 2026-11-01: Task 0.8 (streaming through Vercel) closed. Streaming works on a Vercel preview deployment: a temporary spike route sending 16 SSE-style `tick` events 300 ms apart (about 5 s in total) reached the browser progressively, with no buffering observed. Browser runs as reported by the project owner (first tick, spread; ideal about 300 ms and 4,500 ms): `yarn dev` 931 ms and 4,678 ms; local production (`yarn build && yarn start`) 333 ms and 4,682 ms; Vercel preview 735 ms and 4,491 ms. A direct Node call of the route handler was also incremental. `maxDuration = 10` is set explicitly in the route; Vercel's current docs (read 2026-11-01) give Hobby a 300 s default and maximum with Fluid compute, which they document as enabled by default (the older 10 to 60 s figures are superseded). This was a feasibility spike, not the production design: the stream event contract and client parser (Task 1.8) and `/api/ask` (Task 1.9) remain responsible for the production streaming architecture and must choose their own `maxDuration`. Evidence: `docs/spikes/streaming.md`. Not tested: the production domain, other browsers, POST requests, real LLM streams, enforcement of the 10 s limit, Vercel runtime logs.
- 2026-11-01: Task 0.9 (sample documents) closed. Two synthetic public samples for the fictional company Kestrel & Vane Ltd, in `supabase/seed/`: `kestrel-vane-employee-handbook.pdf` (10 pages, 16 KB, single column, text-based, generated by `build_sample_pdf.py` and committed) and `kestrel-vane-faq.md` (12 questions). Ground truth is `eval/dataset/facts.md`: 52 facts with physical PDF page numbers (7 span two adjacent paragraphs on one page), FAQ restatements, 4 FAQ-only facts, layout markers (tables on pages 1, 4, 6 and 10; lists on pages 2, 3, 5, 6, 7 and 9) and 5 deliberately uncovered topics for Slice 2's refusal questions. `tests/unit/sample-docs.test.ts` checks that every quote is on its stated page and unique in the PDF, that FAQ values match the handbook, and that the uncovered terms appear in neither file. Not decided or tested: the placeholder rows in `supabase/seed.sql` (Northwind, Fabrikam) still use different filenames and are replaced in Task 1.6; chunk counts and how the tables chunk are unknown until Task 1.4; synthetic clean files are a baseline, not a guarantee for real documents.

- 2026-11-01: Task 1.3 (provider interfaces and Gemini adapters) closed. `lib/providers/` holds the `EmbeddingProvider` and `LlmProvider` interfaces and the Gemini adapters. Embeddings: `embed(texts, role)`; the adapter applies the 2026-10-31 embedding decision (`gemini-embedding-2`, 768 dimensions, document prefix `title: none | text: {text}`, query prefix `task: search result | query: {text}`, no `taskType`, no extra L2 normalization), so callers pass raw text and already-prefixed text is rejected; at most 100 texts per call, with no pacing or batching in the adapter (ingestion paces). LLM: `stream(messages)` yields only provider-level `text` and `finish` events; the Task 1.8 application contract is separate. Errors normalize to `RateLimitError`, `ProviderUnavailableError` and `BadRequestError`; 401, 403 and `API_KEY_INVALID` stay `BadRequestError` with `status`, `providerCode`, `providerReason` and `isAuthError` preserved; malformed provider output and mid-stream failures are `ProviderUnavailableError`; raw provider bodies are never kept. Gemini is the only adapter: no Groq adapter or failover was built (Groq stays a spike-tested alternative; failover is Task 5.5). The LLM model is not decided: `gemini-3.5-flash-lite` is the default and streamed successfully in the smoke test, but it remains provisional (see the 2026-10-31 scope entry) and is configurable through the `model` option or `GEMINI_LLM_MODEL`; the Slice 2 eval is where candidates get compared. Verified locally by the project owner (as reported): `yarn install`, lint, typecheck, unit tests and build pass; a temporary Client Component importing `@/lib/providers` failed the build through `server-only` (page deleted afterwards); `yarn provider:smoke` against real Gemini returned 768-dimension, all-finite document and query vectors and a streamed answer ending in a `stop` finish event. Not tested: HTTP 429 and 5xx from the real API (unit tests use mocked responses shaped from Google's documented error format; a real 429 body was never captured), whether an invalid key arrives as 400 `API_KEY_INVALID`, a GitHub Actions run on this change, and behaviour under real ingest load. Spike code was copied, not imported, and `spikes/` is untouched.

- 2026-11-02: Task 1.4 (chunker) closed; recorded together with Task 1.5, so the date is when it was written down. `lib/chunking/` exports `normalizePageText`, `PIPELINE_VERSION`, `chunkPages(pages, options)` and `paginateText(text, options)`, and the `PageInput` (`{ page, text }`), `Chunk` and option types. Normalization lives in one place (`normalize.ts`) and `PIPELINE_VERSION = 1` matches the `documents.pipeline_version` default; changing any normalization rule changes the stored offsets, so the version must be bumped and old documents re-ingested. `normalizePageText` converts CR and CRLF to LF, tabs and NBSP to spaces, collapses space runs and trailing spaces, collapses 3 or more newlines to 2, trims the page, applies NFKC, removes soft hyphens, zero-width and bidi marks, the BOM and control characters (including NUL), keeps ZWJ, ZWNJ and variation selectors, replaces unpaired surrogates with U+FFFD, and is idempotent. `chunkPages` normalizes each page, then returns `Chunk { chunkIndex, pageNumber, charStart, charEnd, content }` where `content === normalizePageText(page.text).slice(charStart, charEnd)` (offsets are UTF-16 code units into the normalized page text, never the raw text). Chunks never cross a page and nothing overlaps across pages; pages keep input order; empty or whitespace-only pages are skipped and `chunkIndex` stays contiguous. Paragraphs are grouped greedily up to `maxChars`; an oversized paragraph falls back to a sentence, then a line, then whitespace boundary, then a hard cut that never splits a surrogate pair. Overlap snaps to a paragraph, sentence or word start and is usually a little smaller than requested, so read it from the offsets; a short final piece is merged when it fits and is never dropped. Defaults are `maxChars` 1200 and `overlapChars` 150 (provisional starting values, not empirically validated; the Slice 2 eval is where they get tuned), with a hard ceiling of 4000 for `chunks.content`. Sizes are in characters, not tokens. Invalid options, duplicate or invalid page numbers throw `RangeError`, and non-string page text throws `TypeError`. `paginateText` cuts `.txt` and `.md` text into virtual pages of about 4000 characters (numbered from 1, normalized first, ending at paragraph boundaries where possible, with no text lost or duplicated); Markdown is not parsed. The 30-page limit is deliberately not enforced in the chunker: extraction (Task 1.5) and ingestion own it. The module has no `server-only` marker on purpose, because the citation viewer needs the same normalization on the client to match chunk text against a rendered PDF text layer. Tests: `tests/unit/chunking-chunk-pages.test.ts`, `chunking-normalize.test.ts` and `chunking-paginate.test.ts` (82 test blocks) cover every Task 1.4 acceptance item plus overlap snapping, Unicode and CJK handling, validation and a generated-input invariants test. Verified: marked done by the project owner; in Claude's sandbox (dependencies installed with npm, fixtures regenerated) the full unit suite passed except `sample-docs.test.ts`, whose seed PDF was missing from the repository snapshot, and lint and typecheck passed for the whole repository. Not tested: chunk counts and chunk quality on the real sample documents (known only once Task 1.6 ingests them, which closes the open note in the 2026-11-01 Task 0.9 entry), retrieval quality, and whether 1200 and 150 are good settings.
- 2026-11-02: Task 1.5 (extraction module) closed. `lib/pdf/` exports `extractPages(buffer: Uint8Array, mimeType: string): Promise<PageInput[]>`, returning the `{ page, text }` shape from `lib/chunking`. PDFs use `unpdf` 1.9.1 as validated in Task 0.6 (spike code was reimplemented, not imported; `spikes/` is untouched): one entry per physical page, 1-based, blank pages kept, text not normalized (the chunker normalizes). `text/plain` and `text/markdown` are decoded as strict UTF-8 and passed to the existing `paginateText()`; Markdown is not parsed or stripped. Checks run in this order: argument types (wrong types throw `TypeError`), size over 5 MiB, zero bytes, MIME type (lowercased, parameters ignored; only `application/pdf`, `text/plain` and `text/markdown`), then content: `%PDF-` within the first 1024 bytes for PDFs (the spike's rule), and for text no NUL bytes (this also rejects UTF-16) and valid UTF-8 (a BOM is stripped; invalid bytes are rejected, never replaced). The MIME type is a routing hint only. Limits (`lib/pdf/limits.ts`, kept local until `lib/config.ts` exists): 30 pages and 5 MiB. The 30-page limit also applies to virtual text pages, because `documents.page_count` is capped at 30 for every type; it is checked before any PDF text is extracted. Errors: every file problem is an `ExtractionError` subclass whose `code` equals a `documents.error_reason` value (`ScannedPdfError` `scanned_pdf`, `TooManyPagesError` `too_many_pages`, `FileTooLargeError` `file_too_large`, `UnsupportedMimeTypeError` and `InvalidFileSignatureError` `unsupported_type`, `CorruptPdfError` `corrupt_file`, `EncryptedPdfError` `encrypted_file`, `EmptyDocumentError` `empty_document`, `UnexpectedExtractionError` `internal_error`). Messages are fixed strings and no `cause` is kept, so raw library output never reaches callers. Only `PasswordException` (encrypted) and `InvalidPDFException`, `MissingPDFException` and `FormatError` (corrupt) get specific codes; any other library error becomes 