sion sees `samples/`, not Alice's files); fixtures are inserted inside T14 and rolled back, with a `where not exists` guard because storage's unique index uses a `COLLATE "C"` expression that `ON CONFLICT` inference cannot match. New: `supabase/migrations/20261107120000_storage.sql`, `lib/storage/documents.ts`, `tests/unit/storage-signed-url.test.ts` (32 tests), `scripts/verify-signed-url.ts` (`yarn verify:signed-url`, local-host guard, service role only to plant and remove one probe file in another user's folder, never prints URLs). Verified (as reported by the project owner, local Supabase): the migration applied; the 32 new tests and the full suite (35 files, 781 tests) passed, with typecheck and lint reporting no errors; `yarn verify:signed-url` passed all 7 checks (demo session anonymous; demo user gets a signed URL for the sample and it serves a PDF with HTTP 200; the probe exists for the service role yet the demo user is refused with `not_found`; no session is refused even for a sample; a malformed path is rejected locally); T14 passed when run on its own (`t14-only.sql`, a temporary file, not committed). Claude's sandbox (no Supabase stack): the new tests, a strict `tsc` check of the helper, tests and script (including a compile-time check that `SupabaseClient<Database>` satisfies the helper's client interface), and two mutation checks (not-found mapping, dots-only file name) that each failed tests as expected. Not tested: the whole `security.sql` script, which still stops at T00d/T00e on a database that has run `yarn seed:samples` (4 public documents instead of the expected 2, the Task 1.7 fixture issue, unchanged) and so needs a fresh `supabase db reset` to run end to end; DATA_MODEL attacks A10, A11 and A14 by curl; the helper called from the browser client; a second real user's file (the verify script used a planted probe in a random user's folder instead); URL expiry behaviour under PDF.js; a hosted project.
- 2026-11-07: Task 3.2 (PDF viewer component) closed. `components/pdf-viewer.tsx` (client) takes `storagePath`, a controlled 1-based `page` and `onPageChange`; `components/pdf-viewer-loader.tsx` loads it with `next/dynamic` and `ssr: false`. It requests the signed URL with the browser session through `createDocumentSignedUrl`. Decisions: (1) PDF.js loads the whole file in one request (`disableRange`, `disableStream`, options kept as a module constant), so the 15-minute signed URL cannot expire mid-session; this settles the Task 3.1 note about range-request failures. (2) Arrow keys, Home and End work only while the viewer has focus, never through a window listener, so typing in the ask box is unaffected; modified keys are ignored. (3) `suspense={false}` on `Document` and `Page`, so load failures become an inline error with Retry instead of reaching the nearest error boundary. (4) Annotation layer off, text layer on (Task 3.5 needs it). (5) Error text and logs use only the PDF.js error name, never its message, because that message can contain the signed URL. Pure helpers are in `lib/pdf-viewer/` with unit tests in `tests/unit/pdf-viewer.test.ts` (12 tests). Temporary page `app/viewer-check/` is the only place the viewer is mounted until Task 3.3, which deletes it. Verified by the project owner: unit tests 12/12, manual checks 13/13, typecheck, lint, `yarn build` and `yarn start` all pass.
- 2026-11-07: Task 3.3 (URL state and layout) closed. A citation chip is now a button that opens the viewer, and the open view lives in the URL as `?doc=<id>&page=<n>&chunk=<id>`. Decisions: (1) `StreamSource` gained a required `chunkId` (`chunks.id`, a positive integer), so the URL can carry `chunk=<id>`. It is set in `toStreamSources` from `match_chunks`' `chunk_id`, and validated in `parseSources` (`lib/ask-stream/events.ts`); a source without it is a protocol error, not skipped. The viewer does not read `chunk` yet: Task 3.5 (highlight) and Task 3.4 (chunk fetch) will. Existing test fixtures that build a `StreamSource` needed the field (`ask-answer-state`, `ask-request-answer`, `ask-stream-parse`, plus the stream, events and route tests). (2) URL state uses the native History API (`pushState` and `replaceState`), which Next.js syncs with `useSearchParams`, so changing the viewer causes no server round trip and no new dependency. Rules, so Back closes the viewer and never walks through pages: opening from closed pushes one entry; switching citation and turning pages replace it. Close and Escape call `history.back()` only if this session pushed the entry, otherwise they rewrite the URL in place (a refreshed page or a shared link has no such entry). "Pushed" is tracked in memory, not in `history.state`, which Next.js owns. (3) The URL is untrusted input: `lib/viewer-url/location.ts` accepts `doc` only as a UUID, `page` only as 1 to 6 digits (the viewer clamps it) and `chunk` only as digits without a leading zero. A `doc` with a malformed value shows an "invalid link" message instead of being ignored; no `doc` means closed. Unrelated query params are kept. (4) `lib/documents/get-viewer-document.ts` resolves the id to title, storage path and mime type with the browser session, so the `documents_select` policy (own or public) decides what is visible; a missing document and another user's look the same ("could not be found, or your session cannot open it"), and a database or network error is a separate state with Retry. Storage access is still decided by the Task 3.1 policy when the signed URL is requested. (5) Layout: one `<aside aria-label="Document viewer">`, a column beside the ask panel from the `lg` breakpoint and a full-height sheet below it; Close and Escape close it, focus moves into the panel on open and returns to the chip on close. The closed home page is unchanged. (6) Text and Markdown documents get a "preview not available yet" message with the title until Task 3.7, so their chips are clickable but do not fail. (7) Known limit: a refresh or a shared link restores the viewer but not the chat answer, which is React state; restoring it would be a separate feature and was not part of this task. New: `lib/viewer-url/location.ts`, `lib/viewer-url/use-viewer-location.ts`, `lib/documents/get-viewer-document.ts`, `components/viewer-panel.tsx`, `components/workspace.tsx`, `tests/unit/viewer-url.test.ts` (23 tests), `tests/unit/viewer-document.test.ts` (4 tests). Changed: `types/ask-stream.ts`, `lib/ask/stream.ts`, `lib/ask-stream/events.ts`, `components/answer-text.tsx`, `components/ask-panel.tsx`, `app/page.tsx` and the test fixtures above. Deleted: the temporary `app/viewer-check/`. Verified by the project owner: all yarn checks passed (the checks were not listed individually) and the manual checks passed; the only thing they noted was the known limit in (7). Claude's sandbox, with only the snapshot files and stand-ins for missing modules: the new tests and the events tests (103), a mutation check on the `chunkId` validation and one on the UUID check (each failed tests as expected), and a scoped strict `tsc` of the changed modules. Not tested: browsers other than the owner's, screen-reader output, a focus trap or scroll lock for the small-screen sheet (not built), and a deployed run.
- 2026-11-07: Task 3.4 (chunk fetch helper) closed. `lib/documents/get-chunk.ts` exports `getChunk(client, chunkId)`, which returns `{ ok: true, chunk }` with `id` (as digits, the form `?chunk=` and `ViewerTarget` use), `documentId`, `pageNumber`, `charStart`, `charEnd` and `content`, or `{ ok: false, reason: "not_found" | "failed" }`. Decisions: (1) It takes a user-scoped client and has no `server-only`, like `getViewerDocument`, so the browser can call it with its own session; never pass the admin client. The `chunks_select` policy (own or public) decides what is visible, so another user's chunk id and a missing one look the same (`not_found`), and a database or network error is a separate `failed` state. (2) The id is validated before any query with a new `isChunkId` in `lib/viewer-url/location.ts`, which reuses the existing `CHUNK_PATTERN` (digits only, no leading zero, at most 15 digits, so always a safe integer); a malformed id returns `not_found` without touching the database. (3) The `select` lists columns explicitly and never includes `embedding` (768 floats the viewer has no use for). (4) The helper does not check that the chunk belongs to the document in `?doc=`: `?doc=A&chunk=<id from B>` is a well-formed link, so the caller (Task 3.5) compares the returned `documentId` and `pageNumber` with what it is showing. (5) `charStart` and `charEnd` are UTF-16 offsets into the normalized text of that page, `charEnd` exclusive (the Task 1.4 convention), so Task 3.5 must match against normalized text. (6) The RLS proof is `supabase/tests/security.sql` T02c to T02f, an extension of T02 rather than a new T number: Alice loads her own and a public chunk by id and gets nothing for Bob's, and the anonymous demo session loads a public chunk but not Alice's. Chunk ids are generated, so the test looks them up as the DB owner and passes them through transaction-local settings, which survive `set local role`; T02c, T02d and the positive half of T02f are the controls that keep the negative checks from passing vacuously. `tests/unit/get-chunk.test.ts` (12 tests) covers the helper's logic only, not RLS. No migration and no UI wiring in this task. Verified by the project owner: `get-chunk` and `viewer-url` unit tests 35/35, `supabase db reset`, `security.sql` all assertions passed (T00 to T10 including T02c to T02f, T11c-d, T17a-d), typecheck, lint, and the full suite 835/835 across 39 files.
- 2026-11-07: Task 3.5 (highlight v1) closed. A citation now highlights its cited passage in the PDF text layer, or declines with a visible reason. Decisions: (1) The matcher moved from `spikes/highlight/match.ts` to `lib/pdf-highlight/match.ts` unchanged in behaviour; `spikes/highlight/outcome.ts` and `tests/unit/spikes-highlight.test.ts` import it from there. (2) The highlight matches `chunk.content` against the page's text layer, not `charStart`/`charEnd`: those are offsets into the server's normalized page text and do not map onto the client's PDF.js items. The offsets are for Task 3.7. (3) Decline rules from the spike are kept (unique match only, 12-character minimum), and one check is added: after the text layer renders, the text inside the `<mark>` elements is compared with the passage, and a mismatch withdraws the highlight (`render_mismatch`). This guards the known fragile spot, the `itemIndex` that react-pdf passes to `customTextRenderer` (it indexes the full `textContent.items`, including marked-content entries without `str`). (4) A highlight shows only when `chunk.documentId` is the document on screen and `chunk.pageNumber` is the page on screen; otherwise there is no highlight and a one-line note. The chunk id comes from the URL, so a well-formed link can name another document's chunk. (5) `getChunk` is called from the browser with the user session (`lib/pdf-highlight/use-chunk-passage.ts`, keyed by chunk id and retry attempt like `useViewerDocument`), so RLS decides visibility and no new endpoint exists. (6) What the viewer reports is a `HighlightStatus` (`lib/pdf-highlight/plan.ts`): none, loading, highlighted, declined with a reason, unavailable, error. `ViewerPanel` shows it as one line above the viewer, with Retry only for a load failure. That line is the hook for the Task 3.6 passage panel, which is not built. (7) The first highlight scrolls into view once per passage, so a window resize does not re-scroll. (8) The whole matched chunk is highlighted, so blocks can be large; this is expected. (9) The files `app/spike/highlight/viewer.tsx` and `observe.ts` that `docs/spikes/highlight.md` describes were never in the git history, so the react-pdf wiring was written from react-pdf 11.0.0's types and source, not copied from the spike; that document is inaccurate on this point. Real-citation result: the handbook citation chips for the first 10 questions in `eval/dataset/qa.json` were clicked, 10 of 10 highlighted the correct chunk, 0 wrong, 0 none (100%). Caveat: the handbook is the only real PDF in the app, so this is one layout. Mixed layouts are covered only by the spike 0.7 numbers (two-column 3 of 5 with the two misses being line-interleaved pages, table 5 of 5), which were measured on synthetic fixtures and not on this code. FAQ chips have no highlight until Task 3.7. New: `lib/pdf-highlight/{match,plan,messages,use-chunk-passage}.ts`, `tests/unit/pdf-highlight.test.ts` (22 tests). Changed: `components/pdf-viewer.tsx`, `components/pdf-viewer-loader.tsx`, `components/viewer-panel.tsx`, `lib/viewer-url/location.ts` (a comment). Verified by the project owner: highlights on the right document and page, highlighted text matching the cited chunk, the failure paths (another page, another document's chunk, a missing chunk, blocked network with Retry), a clean console with no hydration warnings, `yarn typecheck`, `yarn lint`, the full test suite, and the production build and server. Claude's sandbox, with only the snapshot files and stand-ins for missing modules: the new tests plus `viewer-url` and `get-chunk` (57), and a scoped strict `tsc` of the changed modules against react-pdf 11.0.0's types. Not tested: browsers other than the owner's, screen-reader output, the two-column and table layouts through the app (uploads arrive in Slice 4), line-interleaved pages (always declined), a deployed preview, and passages that cross a page break.
- 2026-11-07: Task 3.6 (fallback passage panel) closed. When the highlight is declined because the passage could not be pinpointed on its page, the viewer shows the cited passage as text above the page, labelled as a fallback. Decisions: (1) The panel is shown only for the declines `too_short`, `ambiguous`, `not_found` and `render_mismatch` (`lib/pdf-highlight/fallback.ts`, `selectFallbackPassage`, pure), which can only happen on the page the passage was cut from. It is deliberately not shown for `other_page` (nothing failed; the existing note names the page, and the panel returns when the user is back on that page), for `other_document` (the text would be shown under the wrong document's title), when the chunk did not load (`unavailable`, `error`: there is no text), while a highlight is drawn or being worked out, when the PDF itself failed to load (no page to sit beside; its own error and Retry apply), or when the passage text is empty. (2) The task's own acceptance case is a unit test: an edited passage makes `planHighlight` decline (no ranges, so nothing can be highlighted) and the selector then returns the edited text; a post-render mismatch (`render_mismatch`) also shows the panel. (3) The passage is rendered as escaped React text (`whitespace-pre-wrap`), never as HTML, because it comes from a document; it is selectable (no `select-none`). The scroll box is capped at `max-h-56` and focusable (`tabIndex={0}`) so keyboard users can scroll a chunk of up to 4000 characters. (4) Accessible label: a `<section>` labelled by its `h3` ("Cited passage, page N"), plus a caption saying it is shown as text because it could not be pinpointed. (5) The Task 3.5 status note above the viewer is unchanged, so the note and the caption both say the passage could not be pinpointed; kept to avoid touching `messages.ts` and its tests, and easy to trim. (6) No new data fetching, endpoint, dependency or migration: the panel reuses the chunk `ViewerPanel` already loads for the highlight. New: `lib/pdf-highlight/fallback.ts`, `components/passage-fallback.tsx`, `tests/unit/pdf-fallback.test.ts` (12 tests). Changed: `components/viewer-panel.tsx` (the selector call and one rendered line). Verified by the project owner in the browser (as reported; the page used in the accessibility check was page 3): a normal citation highlights with no panel; the forced mismatch (`chunk.content + " EDITED"`) shows the full passage and no highlight; the text is selectable and can be copied; the panel can be focused and scrolled with the keyboard; the accessibility inspector shows role `region` with the name "Cited passage, page 3"; Next removes the panel and the note names the cited page, and Previous restores it; a chunk of a different document shows no panel and the "belongs to a different document" note; after reverting the temporary edit, normal highlighting works again. Claude's sandbox, with only the snapshot files and a minimal install (vitest, typescript): the new tests and the existing `pdf-highlight` tests (34 passed), two mutation checks (also showing the panel for `other_page`: 1 test failed; dropping `render_mismatch`: 2 failed) and a strict `tsc` of `fallback.ts` and its test. Not reported or not run: `yarn typecheck`, `yarn lint`, `yarn format:check`, the full suite and `yarn build` for this change; `components/passage-fallback.tsx` and `components/viewer-panel.tsx` were not typechecked in Claude's sandbox. Not tested: browsers other than the owner's, a screen reader (only the accessibility inspector), the panel on a small-screen sheet with a very long passage (layout not specifically checked), a natural decline on a real PDF (the decline was forced by editing the passage; the handbook's own chips all highlighted in Task 3.5), and a deployed preview. Not built: the text and Markdown viewer and its highlight by `char_start` and `char_end` (Task 3.7), so those documents still have no passage panel.
